# Reason: A game that was just added has loose .rpy files with no valid .rpyc. Script.load_file parses them one after the other on one core: 76 percent of a cold load of a large script after lazy compilation. Forked workers now compile the files first (renpy/parallelcompile.py), in waves that end at every file with a python early block or an early config value (renpy/scriptscan.py reads the text for them), and write the same .rpyc files into the cache. Script.load_script then loads every file in the same order as before and finds a valid .rpyc for each, so node order, init priorities, the namemap, the duplicate label check and the early blocks are those of a serial load. At most 4 workers and two cores stay free; they are reaped when the load ends or stops early. Where fork is missing (Windows), with fewer than two workers or for little source the load stays serial. RENPY_PARALLEL_COMPILE=N sets the number of workers (0 turns it off).

